prompt:
The timetable office of a city bus operator, on the morning the stop plates for route 27 go to the printers. What is wanted is the plate for one stop, SPRING ROAD -- the sheet a passenger reads standing at the pole.

What the schedule for route 27 says:
-- the first bus leaves the terminus at 06:00 and the last one leaves at 10:30;
-- from 06:00 until 08:00 one bus leaves the terminus every 20 minutes;
-- from 08:00 until 09:00 one leaves every 10 minutes;
-- from 09:00 onwards one leaves every 30 minutes;
-- the running time from the terminus to SPRING ROAD is 12 minutes.

The plate gives the times buses reach SPRING ROAD, one row to each hour, the hour at the left-hand end of its own row and that hour's minutes running across it, and at the foot the number of buses that call here in the day.

Work all of that out for yourself -- I am deliberately not telling you how many buses call in any hour, which row the service thins out on, or what the day comes to. A plate that runs at the same rate from top to bottom is no use to anybody at the pole. Nothing on the plate may give the working away: no headway note, no table of the bands, no arrows. Every hour row and the figure at the foot must be readable in the frame.
